[{"id":"VRCE-2-47-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":1,"english":"Having taken refuge under the tree and performed his evening devotions, that foremost of those capable of charming others. Rāma, addressed Laksmana, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0a2f03a061525a2bd2939e4a54e4e80bc0fb0694b5dfb2c82b39946d3d92e979","compact":"74bdb55de3ab4e1f5d452e55281b19e9c871c224a70d4a78e116db535199008a","nasal":"74bdb55de3ab4e1f5d452e55281b19e9c871c224a70d4a78e116db535199008a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":2,"english":"To day is the first night (which we must spend) outside the inhabited tracts without the company of Sumantra.. You ought not to suffer your mind to be uneasy on that score.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a6c9e7182332f47f7fece5a4c189ce119eb65ac3e56e733b44176fae0685b77b","compact":"63f6ba90ca93569c33eb59e70b905c67d4f70f534d254fb2cbbc34f18a7c3bec","nasal":"81f42d7872208350c5d7f03e25466d111390ed0197acc7e31c6114820964e7c7"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":3,"english":"From to night forth, we shall have to guard her vigilantly, for, O Laksmana, the preservation of what she has as well as the securing to her of what she has not, rests with us.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.779,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e36a0b0bc1a262394d850dfc2fb05b381dd3a2f42407b7a690c9967ef69a9fd3","compact":"f5eb32e38039a0583d3fa8a40003d5f69fec0df65d611374a21ff8382a486c9a","nasal":"6fb6ae47f163625a38dc13881673d31d81fd0f0a2c1a753867570c93bdfda827"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":4,"english":"We will, O son of Sumitrā, anyhow pass the night; let us ourselves procuring (leaves) and spreading them on the ground, anyhow lie down on it.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.727,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ad827247428ce9994001c3c14991c4e42dc74c4bece41a3958b9e4856cf166bc","compact":"162ca28a12924eb77c5535a9150a883f411f1ed1f94e93a476f6b190424caad4","nasal":"162ca28a12924eb77c5535a9150a883f411f1ed1f94e93a476f6b190424caad4"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":5,"english":"Saying this, Rāma lying down on the ground although worthy of a costly bed, spoke these excellent words to Sumitrā's son.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8c19ce54378221b9f267dbc7d1923145b3a631e76346bb8591c6afdc5108cb66","compact":"41085a0c57f821e824ab739c6cf9e34f3e8f465d456d0ce6178e87cd00043720","nasal":"41085a0c57f821e824ab739c6cf9e34f3e8f465d456d0ce6178e87cd00043720"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":6,"english":"O Lakşmaņa, surely the king sleeps uneasily to day, and Kaikeyi having attained her end ought to be satisfied.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0eb514cb12962a121c65e6cb0df61d36a09cb0b345ed8a367d0752f70084e2e2","compact":"99a6bd39b739e93ea60d8fa46387efd57584a5e9ce1663e831a0c16f74c7a629","nasal":"99a6bd39b739e93ea60d8fa46387efd57584a5e9ce1663e831a0c16f74c7a629"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":7,"english":"Will not that revered lady, for the purpose of having Bharata established in the kingdom, take the king's life, when she shall see Bharata arrived?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.875,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8eccdd10dd40231adfba9ce5e4262859d370c9b8b026025dd50c76f59c54a2b8","compact":"ed5ffa3e0b66ac87da3a8bcbd3522209907385a0504d45ccc7105b7fd42d1046","nasal":"2d7489762543ad46fb33f2f25d3ca313805ca596db34f2ac58dc8bc14d28d2fc"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":8,"english":"Forlorn and old and deprived of me. I do not know what he will do, his soul possessed by desire, and having come under the influence of Kaikeyi.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.845,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"557102063461d601783d72d6f4ba6ab9bc78e42e3cd0f85d47cc3668789f0834","compact":"88aa74df6f8d22b16be765fb8c87baabd74abb0f303c71dfa24dcc827317a58d","nasal":"88aa74df6f8d22b16be765fb8c87baabd74abb0f303c71dfa24dcc827317a58d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":9,"english":"Viewing this calamity that has overtaken us) and the disorder that has taken place in the senses of the monarch, I deem even lust as more potent than either virtue or interest.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.869,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7e8c794b3c8adcee278965f0a77bea01006a9e2728ce19468ccd55aee794cecf","compact":"2124b522084ce82369e0cc3164496ea6993ee676b15fd67175d1660eaee64528","nasal":"2124b522084ce82369e0cc3164496ea6993ee676b15fd67175d1660eaee64528"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":10,"english":"O Lakşmana, what man is there ignorant though he be, who for the sake of a female forsake as my father has done me, his son following his foot-steps?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ff108944d86e5136304a1ccea0a9e955e1045aa52e474759aa77a661e06c1358","compact":"542fa63b5fd4845224a1f8d79c31fb565f8309ba297ea15d20ca427178a975e5","nasal":"6c054bbf8d3e5e67e400eccf1646f5c1c8995f5bbd6430442e14f40f4e8f8a48"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":11,"english":"Ah! Kaikeyi's son Bharata with his wife is really happy, he that enjoys the sole sovereignty of the delighted Kosalas.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5192e7c1208c07288bc0d319c8b7a139c06b871bccb1cfa8f493e90c7395c505","compact":"f52659c5a5bb11e9fd742bd52b4cd16206c56347846b86ce39eb556b1f52cfc6","nasal":"f52659c5a5bb11e9fd742bd52b4cd16206c56347846b86ce39eb556b1f52cfc6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":12,"english":"Now that our father has grown old and I have taken refuge in the forest, he will alone experience the supreme felicity in the kingdom.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.536,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"494845c584a61a57f45fb571f3f4e49b3ae44781ec45e4290637d3e332e7dcac","compact":"9cce9ea63e1e5f1209b7dcd685fc38a27526aa715a0da26570198da564b2e3e5","nasal":"9cce9ea63e1e5f1209b7dcd685fc38a27526aa715a0da26570198da564b2e3e5"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":13,"english":"He that renouncing interest and virtue, follows lust, speedily gets himself involved in troubles even like king Dasaratha.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.855,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ab110baf9fe1f4739404a01d1e037e5621fc9f16c48e0851beef4a53e9230b45","compact":"18c474e417a2d89c9bfe29184e885965a01f1f5ef98ea9a3a8aef44d323fb341","nasal":"18c474e417a2d89c9bfe29184e885965a01f1f5ef98ea9a3a8aef44d323fb341"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":14,"english":"O amiable one, I think that Kaikeyi has been born for making an end of Dasaratha, sending me into exile, and conferring the kingdom on Bharata.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.86,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3f6bd42cdb1bdcf172cff1236df19b204cdfd92b335c4dff9082c2b47f63e3d2","compact":"c20280bb2a793a5fbd0cd7c64feacfc7243526e2fe6113271a6be6af5e7c0afb","nasal":"c30237706e32c1c4ecb4f13954823307a04bbdac5189ce32bcaf35b68ad635e9"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":15,"english":"At present for impareing me pain, Kaikeyi intoxicated by the tide of good fortune, will afflict Kausalya and Sumitrā.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.729,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cabef9990ef72a45a4953ca007501b9c28ff95d6022938bfd7f213ac4e658835","compact":"e958bb1c7506ff95f463416b0c265be867f951350ca4133b60fa31099bc01166","nasal":"e958bb1c7506ff95f463416b0c265be867f951350ca4133b60fa31099bc01166"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":16,"english":"Your mother, the reversed Sumitrā, will be smitten with grief on our account. Do you, Laks maņa, tomorrow morning repair to Ayodhyā:","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.922,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4febcaaf8a3ec77cf1e6396505ae7e67c197983f57ee5bad2deba211ca5b8de7","compact":"eaf0f0c52b7ba74a96ed47618be48fbdba1590336aa8a746cfdefb2716a4ec43","nasal":"eaf0f0c52b7ba74a96ed47618be48fbdba1590336aa8a746cfdefb2716a4ec43"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":17,"english":"I alone will go to Dandaka along with Sītā. You will be the protector of the helpless Kausalya.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1560899f356e507a8b721789e10568a567a92aff6d56a05012dd5725a92d85c1","compact":"d09642505956c4a303297fd864b83c1f1cb08e9d7314f49095ffbb7d666be6d1","nasal":"cc0a05f3511acf8c83048a9642043a34ead30a3ab717389e663a4b44f816d11b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":18,"english":"Kaikeyi is surely mean-minded, she perpetrates wrongs from malice. O you cognizant of virtue, she may administer poison to my mother.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.594,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c5541859d68e8e145820208d3cafcb434ad99f37d80463198b3790c61919a581","compact":"a5f982efa74a6bb7b9d132a31a164266a0dfc5dec080ca9ff18732b0c6dd46cf","nasal":"a5f982efa74a6bb7b9d132a31a164266a0dfc5dec080ca9ff18732b0c6dd46cf"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":19,"english":"Surely, O child, in a former birth, women were bereft of their sons by my mother, O son of Sumitrā; and it is for this that this misfortune has befallen her.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.662,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a8768f8f7d1ef1c5cfd6ac7a04e38a080760c06456dd116661fced529c492a70","compact":"a930dc86e82b0cdffc64174e1369e3fa0621231d7d1e67738c3cf3a2430feb53","nasal":"181af297752d8a35dc22345007fdeb24940cfd86d8f979c8d4e2d20f619bc6ec"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":20,"english":"Having been brought up and reared with great pains by Kausalyä, I have left her at the time when her labours ought to have borne fruit, Fie on me.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"23cbb5cb2e54b8ab0a35ae9d0abfeed6519c85cc5d7ffe1bb377342a276ad628","compact":"e2ff94c8767b67d5fbd1883fd5902c8297543943a915f99d24d3a5329543b494","nasal":"e2ff94c8767b67d5fbd1883fd5902c8297543943a915f99d24d3a5329543b494"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":21,"english":"Let on woman, O son of Sumitrā, gibe birth to a son like me who have imparted such infinite pain to my mother.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"96621975eed82051f91059b69aceefefa958ba169fd0e483bb8dab7c13141795","compact":"3330568e4461fa9aaf887b997c1ff9aa0232cdead79f60685118c1d66dc54bb4","nasal":"7ea9e0f28fef6aa4b40bf6f43bfff48bcc9948d7258314723b02287038619884"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":22,"english":"O Laksmana, I consider my mother's female parrot as more sharing her affection, since she is heard to say, 'O Śuka, do you bite the foot of the foe?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.75,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"021db2d1838afaad99f5110624e789b068b0f26b7bece32cfa5968611df29e03","compact":"54fda056bc7ac3069e9b7eff29c9193aeb620321712965d3845279deb246c0d3","nasal":"54fda056bc7ac3069e9b7eff29c9193aeb620321712965d3845279deb246c0d3"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":23,"english":"What am I, O repressor of foes, now to do for her, bewailing, of slender fortune, she that has not profited in the least by her son, and who stands in no further need of his good offices ? Surely my unfortunate mother, Kausalyā, bereft of me, lies down on the ground, overwhelmed with woe, and plunged in an ocean of grief..","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.896,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"508bb84a60a611c0e4d56150fefd889f85f6f3826ca984e1aae18bd0002a3ebd","compact":"b0730c9e5180b319310b372c0c4c22e061a65b889e3ed88f841703e35b1d0e49","nasal":"abf2b688c03f1b16f4b80d5188fdd3e3c11117cfc7b6722e3816d3c4c8c68948"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":24,"english":"What am I, O repressor of foes, now to do for her, bewailing, of slender fortune, she that has not profited in the least by her son, and who stands in no further need of his good offices ? Surely my unfortunate mother, Kausalyā, bereft of me, lies down on the ground, overwhelmed with woe, and plunged in an ocean of grief..","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d85e5d8a16492293dec3648ea868a24bdc0e8e6c05210401c5c51076443a8015","compact":"05ab81058bb2c5742442a54f89e38dfcaf957c5c5d39c8aa18a955024cb0594d","nasal":"05ab81058bb2c5742442a54f89e38dfcaf957c5c5d39c8aa18a955024cb0594d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-25","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":25,"english":"O Laksmana, enraged, I alone without doubt, can rid Ayodhyā, the Earth herself, by means of my arrows. But improper is the display of prowess for no reason,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047025","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d80f1286d0740878ecd9de5841c234e56b1e52dc4449551a9de2b79630369852","compact":"995dd50679c0328f856a53a1bf42013cd99ec8609c341a313ad91f8f9273c5e6","nasal":"995dd50679c0328f856a53a1bf42013cd99ec8609c341a313ad91f8f9273c5e6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-26","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":26,"english":"O sinless one, I am afraid of unrighteousness and of the next life; and for this it is that, O Lakşmaņa, I do not install myself in the kingdom.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047026","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ac6c387d94fcc4584caa5bf2c0e8aab67edb72a42e9d1c1e5f93018823f50190","compact":"f796f4d381943c603a4a73e030233c6c00214c2e68f2ed769cde19e0dbff0fea","nasal":"f796f4d381943c603a4a73e030233c6c00214c2e68f2ed769cde19e0dbff0fea"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-27","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":27,"english":"Having in solitude for a long while piteously bewailed thus and in other ways, Rāma sat silent in the night with tears in his eyes.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.828,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047027","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2019af2fb64f86f02ed0c7d5ea0c897b38c530729fa7a62f0d0efed4f0f5370e","compact":"e427f67a898d9a886226dfa026bccddce517a617a0c746cd2223dc11f4f0181b","nasal":"e427f67a898d9a886226dfa026bccddce517a617a0c746cd2223dc11f4f0181b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-28","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":28,"english":"Thereupon Lakşmaņa consoled Rāma spent with lamentation, like to fire deprived of its radiance or the ocean of its tide.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.895,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047028","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"965be1f709fe87ec1cf4d84ede03348ac737d21cc4684390ecd30182f6475e3e","compact":"afe98e9b17f274c62b3f7aab04481832a8c5f83ab4a82615b2af7be9276da999","nasal":"afe98e9b17f274c62b3f7aab04481832a8c5f83ab4a82615b2af7be9276da999"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-29","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":29,"english":"Surely, O Rama, O foremost of warriors, on your having come out, the city of Ayodhyā is shorn of its splendour like the night deprived of the moon.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.881,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-28"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047029","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"19bc51f3a52a117ac445245c8023c66b10d07e817fc7f13caa69ee4288af1c2e","compact":"c71fcad511a38eccddabdd47cfb675ebfc49859e3c8ebbaeba663bc293fdcbd7","nasal":"85f858cd1e76026d216612caf94fbb3bfab9388cc3bd99528f00f1b2c825a831"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-30","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":30,"english":"This is not fit that you should grieve; for thereby, O foremost of men, you make both Sītā and myself grieve.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.898,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047030","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d739493cfa5f16fbcfde009f97fe18385d8242b50c047db32b6ce74f3136bf2d","compact":"0dc9fef3af0386bd317decc4c4b63117e70b36ccc0361f16d1ed65838d3e5b94","nasal":"0dc9fef3af0386bd317decc4c4b63117e70b36ccc0361f16d1ed65838d3e5b94"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-31","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":31,"english":"O Rāghava, deprived of you neither Sītā nor I can live for a moment, like fish taken out of water : without you.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.947,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-30"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047031","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"170b85ccde9b7072b1282c9ff9d205f9826209b7dea2a4a55c4a3cab9d94891d","compact":"5eff57248f9288da8d1c9b67006235298c426f98c82f6e0bea1e3bea2ba8d101","nasal":"5eff57248f9288da8d1c9b67006235298c426f98c82f6e0bea1e3bea2ba8d101"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-32","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":32,"english":"O repressor of foes, I wish to see neither my father, nor Satrughna, nor Sumitrā, nor heaven itself.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-31"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047032","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"952b7448825d5e16372c41417e436916586504a3c329ad757d00ca0bd79ae982","compact":"0cbe92fd6c9f80f78d5e194f6036a7912ce3f04be0a84de1ae92c19830aef3bc","nasal":"0cbe92fd6c9f80f78d5e194f6036a7912ce3f04be0a84de1ae92c19830aef3bc"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-47-33","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":47,"verse":33,"english":"Hearing Lakşmaņa's excellent and appropriate words with which he gladly assumed a life in the woods, that subduer of foes, Rāghava, in the name of righteousness, at once fully granted him the permission to dwell with him for the entire fourteen years.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.907,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-33"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2047033","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"054245fa3c6b6437c88becf9bb944e820ecb05e6c44517bbb13f4354e5144469","compact":"a5c8a54cec120bc266a9e69638a0d9bb9bc21a7305fb4fbdf608d3b14114af96","nasal":"a5c8a54cec120bc266a9e69638a0d9bb9bc21a7305fb4fbdf608d3b14114af96"}}]
